\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{A compatible sequence completes in resistance}
\date{2026-11-07}

\p{Let #{(V_n,L_n,E_n)} be a
\vocabk{compatible resistance sequence}{fdip-00EG}. Set
##{F_*=\bigl\{u:V_*\to\mathbb R:
\sup_n E_n(u|_{V_n})<\infty\bigr\}}
and #{E_*(u,u)=\lim_n E_n(u|_{V_n})}, with the bilinear form
obtained by polarization. For #{a,b\in V_*} set
#{R(a,a)=0} and, when #{a\ne b},
##{R(a,b)=\sup\frac{|u(a)-u(b)|^2}{E_*(u,u)},}
the supremum running over #{u\in F_*} with positive energy.
Let #{X} be the completion of #{V_*} in #{R}. Then
#{(E_*,F_*)} is a resistance form on #{V_*}, and it extends
uniquely to a resistance form on #{X} whose resistance
metric is the completed #{R}. Functions in the extended
domain are continuous on #{X}. The quotient by constants is
a Hilbert space, the unit contraction does not increase the
energy, and the trace of the extended form on each #{V_n}
is #{E_n}. Compactness of #{X}, a measure, and a Laplacian
are not concluded.}

\proof{
\p{\ref{fdip-00EH} places the operators #{-L_n} under
\citet{Theorem 3.13}{kigami2010resistance}. That theorem
supplies the resistance form on #{V_*}, the identification
of #{R} with its resistance metric, and the unique
continuous extension to the completion #{X}. The Hilbert
property of the quotient by constants and the unit
contraction are conditions of the resistance form in
\citet{Definition 3.1}{kigami2010resistance}, which the
theorem provides. The same theorem returns the trace on
#{V_n} to #{-L_n}, hence to #{E_n}. That trace is the
finite-point interpolation: boundary values on #{V_n} have
a unique energy-minimizing extension, by
\citet{Proposition 3.10}{kigami2010resistance}. The
hypotheses named in those statements are the finite sets,
the Laplacian conditions, and the exact trace, all checked
in \ref{fdip-00EH}.}
}
